#{[#edb/ref 17592186048780 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097486} :activity/id #uuid "90bcccea-5782-59d1-a524-d6852abe26cd" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097837 :tutorial/content "Given a fixed **origin** in the plane (often denoted ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg)), and another point ![](images/42/42193b6382254991fb9c053ead9edd0631767155ea51d8a8551cfc4fc734340e.svg) the vector ![](images/20/20edf7e26e0b35148bf8ff1e9b52c38e5b6672ae0462a8c2e218e1c0456bae07.svg) is called the **position vector** of the point ![](images/ed/ed6dc1fd057e3dd2207c735a562bf4db22b72d1a9380651607439f00c0440725.svg)\n\nSo, the position vector tells us how to move from the origin to the point.\n\nFor example, suppose ![](images/4f/4f9204dcee76e03f03dc64827bf810327c9b2b981a0aee5a10c7f80ca98f4d5d.svg) and ![](images/41/412bd151cdf5602575028591e2f87d9a7e0328aa6b18dccb6459e7041a5d9b9c.svg) How can we find the position vector ![](images/20/20edf7e26e0b35148bf8ff1e9b52c38e5b6672ae0462a8c2e218e1c0456bae07.svg) in terms of ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) and ![](images/c5/c5d2e1827ba6a11504655381514c15ce7eba142e62bf8dca3a10d12eef9d7861.svg)\n\n![](images/e6/e61003fb62534b04cfbbac07a64e61f6c16e2d464eb35bc5b98654b0603b24db.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we can trace a path from ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg) to ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) by first moving from ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg) to ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) and then from ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) to ![](images/ed/ed6dc1fd057e3dd2207c735a562bf4db22b72d1a9380651607439f00c0440725.svg) Therefore,\n![](images/ac/ac96d55fd0d74cf5fd24f1f1a96beb3865bc70bc45d17be5aad209c5adb1be6d.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/4f/4f9204dcee76e03f03dc64827bf810327c9b2b981a0aee5a10c7f80ca98f4d5d.svg) and ![](images/e2/e283cb6e7ab325c89dd652abd93ff1382b714462887d8839dfed8769693cc7a7.svg) we get\n![](images/e1/e1bde038ef63f77c3ecc5357b49999c96dd6d331aa7455fcdd1935b1f3eb8261.svg)\n\nSo, to find a position vector, we can trace a convenient path from the origin to the point and add the vectors along that path." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362445}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096652 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096451 :question/id #uuid "dccb5bac-e528-5b42-9949-36b3870fdde1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4287" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n![](images/b8/b8abf22d14f93a38c4d01f368b8171e4ccfe90086d932f5b58453baa46391405.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/76/76b9867a7c1ef484922241ebe44272f0104e406c31310bf36227a13850f50d8b.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup ONM$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $ON=2|\\mathbf{n}|$;\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{ON}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{ON}=2\\mathbf{n}$.\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $NM=2|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{NM}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{NM}=2\\mathbf{k}$.\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\overset{\\to }{ON}+\\overset{\\to }{NM} \\\\  & =2n+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}=2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}$. \n\nSince $\\mathbf{n} \\nparallel \\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=2$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097488}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096653 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096452 :question/id #uuid "09d593cc-526e-5691-9a18-efff515eae59" :question/math-academy-id "e-4288" :question/problem "Consider the following diagram, where $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b},$ and $X$ divides $AB$ in the ratio $3:1.$ Find $\\overrightarrow{OX}.$\n\n![](images/67/678ea2936b8a5b1ded74cb8a0875c629e8be0f7002c98492ebf2e8109fc603ef.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overrightarrow{AX}.$\n\nFirst, from $\\triangle OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AX:XB=3:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AX}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\dfrac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AX} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{3}{4}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}a+\\frac{3}{4}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096701 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096495 :question/id #uuid "839ed5f5-165f-5de5-aba1-551ac605475f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5212" :question/problem "Consider the picture below.\n\n![](images/f2/f2518c2e998e7c7f551548a4cc7283720a66428ea8fa9872494a81cc3848ba9e.png)\n\nGiven that ![](images/c6/c6714d0a6eaa5c7d354d46e8d6d60da7f3d2011df0fc75773036cf7ae510c5e3.png), find ![](images/24/24878591902884f45f14f5c447bc326586a411a8870b205fc12825a4ab2c738a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when two known vectors are not parallel, a linear combination of them is unique. So, once we rewrite ![](images/5c/5c644b15efcf5a4274de23242a722604ead2ff800ea29ba7a650729227fefdf0.png) in terms of ![](images/27/27f36b1d1846e12e3c50b7d4e47f5745727792d52a48e4d85b689ec36e3d25a0.png) and ![](images/a7/a7b9033c421072576aaf959980d778c319a8ed0f849e08db644b63bc6994088c.png) we can match coefficients to determine the unknown scalar.\n\nTo find ![](images/16/166c90a9c29f99347bff6b397f31d705ec6407a19b4d71246f98308bbd7a5473.png) we want to match the expression\n![](images/fc/fc79367916dd7671c17de4f40b3d83f91d41c2f9988645964de0b53e5b11ab9b.png)\n\nWe are given that one of the components is ![](images/91/91f686c6ca02e85c386cb0f9e80b66beface446d3a541952e0ac91738ad0db37.png) So, we first draw the vector ![](images/d5/d54b9a8919c7b6106fcf5770c62b1823605d7be98a55d5333e73bcfb7528997a.png) from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point ![](images/6e/6e1bbbe9eb66856824ff70d16cc2c077519d361546b226a67146e20f15a24dd0.png)\n\n![](images/ea/ea517ac4ca8e0bcf7ba48f23e66f100323d5485f1221219aacfbea9148f4a1af.png)\n\nNote that in ![](images/f1/f1c944e6afa4e9cd84bc68004a2bfd918d81498a727aeb7c56ec627806bf93f6.png), we have:\n\n- ![](images/b7/b7ac390c4e43b2fa2fbb4063632f98869256cea877caa5e5dad4aad33ae94722.png)\n\n- ![](images/7e/7e5f2b01647ab01052fff18ab760273eebe19d47b56051b1953adf587d7f9fb3.png) and ![](images/78/784225d11bf94b7fbd67f8e2395695141eb75b1cb44f89d49b29bb3a2031534b.png) have the same direction\n\nHence, ![](images/21/21261c8ad0d86a9e7300ffc58c57fd76beef9f627492bcfe2051d5e3cfd82a4e.png)\n\nAdditionally,\n![](images/ae/ae9c98ded3aa4317a729571710ad38d973a2d67b5faeb9ad26af7c24ee5c3193.png)\n\nConsequently, ![](images/ae/aee3287ad64a654880216142756811bd4d5232b3dba2f48378b8b1c5241e4316.png)\n\nSince ![](images/46/466863f8c7522ac89896a4233a823be02940535097c28328fa2c9e9e1f4bc3d8.png) the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of ![](images/37/3720c5887713d3f7a29be384fe5e883390935b2b9b469af626a8dba968f4da8b.png) we get:\n![](images/4a/4ac145b710c5354b44a4ea1dc820d70f54b5c17985f078fce6313b671ec26fd9.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362445 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362404 :tutorial/content "Now let's look at a slightly more complicated diagram.\n\nSuppose we have two vectors ![](images/4f/4f9204dcee76e03f03dc64827bf810327c9b2b981a0aee5a10c7f80ca98f4d5d.svg) and ![](images/92/92e98c3630891023a50149ee1f15d2bd5d2730e93f166931e86a9e4640ebd1c1.svg) and a parallelogram ![](images/14/14fa5b1a9ad8e22445d2e6ab97a047b0b6dc51912ed129823847560bad78554f.svg) where ![](images/37/37252d39c3cb979bda5aa0e19184009625e32ad6b4d496ec843edcd9ebd11444.svg) How can we find the position vector ![](images/1e/1ea9147123ec18b9ae6f584584c919a462f7efdee6b5b72b61a1cf3c4b13b88a.svg) in terms of ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) and ![](images/c5/c5d2e1827ba6a11504655381514c15ce7eba142e62bf8dca3a10d12eef9d7861.svg)\n\n![](images/c7/c78c8895790b28c7dbe60d0dd31a5375fef19ba2d6fa668144859e6fafaf33bb.png)\n\nAs before, we trace a path from the origin to the point ![](images/ed/ed6dc1fd057e3dd2207c735a562bf4db22b72d1a9380651607439f00c0440725.svg) From the diagram, we can write\n![](images/8c/8c0197f45331c0bfd19524c6044f35ddd1d725512112d9700750d10c8709f89f.svg)\n\nWe know that\n![](images/14/146676b86f179710731d5e1ec1b4e5e5e3cf4cdf1af0d4cd4db3d709a36ea0c6.svg)\n\nAlso, since ![](images/c6/c6dbfaa7b99647ca21ec1ad2c57f563756ed17cec04b559d89a086571f1419a1.svg) is a parallelogram, opposite sides are equal and parallel. So,\n![](images/94/940eac51696aa93c499bd105cf5726bbed6eaac94eda297d0743af6cd00a67d3.svg)\n\nSubstituting these into the expression for ![](images/a6/a69eca8aae37a4ecd6d3653c994b2bea69e37a8be6dcb00c4a5eeafbc58f1382.svg) we get\n![](images/f5/f56355c56683deba996e3f8cbdd8a9862cd7a9f6f570be9ab12cd4520748e7ec.svg)\n\nSo, even when the path uses translated copies of known vectors, we can still find the position vector by expressing each part of the path in terms of the known vectors and then adding.\n\nNow we will use the same path-tracing idea in a slightly different way. Instead of only writing a position vector, we will also use vector paths to identify unknown coefficients in linear combinations of known vectors." :tutorial/title "Writing a Position Vector Using Known Vectors"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362403 :tutorial/content "We can still find a position vector by tracing a path from the origin to the point and adding the vectors along that path.\n\nHowever, if one part of the path points in the opposite direction to a known vector, then we write it as a **negative** multiple of that vector.\n\nFor example, consider the picture below. \n\n![](images/d5/d5366a68ed98e7f614ff998f69098613ea8e12d05bc27274ea66e8ce5a908cb7.png)\n\nWe are given that ![](images/80/80eb0b356da6e6a2315ea1466d34b914c6cad5e59ff07c4d061ea82b69ed9838.svg) and we are asked to find ![](images/44/44db815dcaf0a9d405026460a655495e055df7e55a785f542f8920ca90af5418.svg)\n\nTo find ![](images/4a/4a2f3891f0b4c8b852767d556e1607028035219b70ab83b4910e95159a930b9c.svg) we want to match the expression\n![](images/52/52599a11cea864ea801b87d8ec0ea288252afa4265395d9fbb4a4dc2815e9e7e.svg)\n\nLet ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg) be the origin, and let ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) and ![](images/a1/a14e933631f2b075c5a0bb4ab14fad2ca2790c3be2122b1fe8f4b7193fb505b2.svg) end at points ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and ![](images/21/2109f0ae1a3679002930f1bf8bfa3490cf23c540ae6bbd50c093e5064327ab15.svg) as shown in the diagram below.\n\nSince one term in the vector sum is ![](images/bd/bdd7762354dbbdecb02ccf8284e0830f5421588bbb7fbf1bd9c3a9268a750649.svg) we first draw the vector ![](images/cb/cbbad9d9d0e183ac112cfdf946b99fd00718e0c6856d1a75564925b0e2760e85.svg) from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point ![](images/27/278f99df1e14fe0ebfb61bdb72262a62670dcaa60d322148bc3ee591b278ec2d.svg)\n\n![](images/bc/bc976b12b4610c69953545d95662bf5535ef2a92eb9d9cbd63e087c85f3b98e7.png)\n\nLet ![](images/1a/1aaddf0d4be4d873ff6c0600b0b239e0565a05ca8daaff7ca92bd5c70bebcf03.svg) be the endpoint of the vector ![](images/a2/a2b2f82b89941e4065ecf4c3bf612ab3d7e66fdb16c848dd2ce169480714e7ae.svg) Then\n\n![](images/3a/3a9a2959c3a256cb2651e0f00b0d7fe774f95aeee92289284bffb4c3b11b843a.svg)\n\nFrom the construction, we have\n\n![](images/f1/f1e5980924989b13362a77d776ecbd4b7567f99e30fe26eb57ee99d18fa1d464.svg)\n\nAlso, the vector ![](images/5f/5f1f2cecb3a462fc45e60ec717c2fde5b1aaba79448a58aae1a9d3023b21b7bb.svg) has length ![](images/f5/f551620762fb84e33026129595b42ef514082cc2e137b6762d75fd574d56383c.svg) but points in the opposite direction to ![](images/85/853e57b0bf9a8f7c16e59d37575ae23ac4028ed66b30720af9d7d49ab595c91b.svg) Therefore,\n\n![](images/4f/4fdd6b0f88ff6e5ea11ace72a5b61c24fe8cf6b1d85b4bceee599dde18471b5d.svg)\n\nSubstituting these into the expression for ![](images/3c/3cce8321317b15087f61b778b47963fdff160e7df1e3ade0d33b040e10125994.svg) we get\n\n![](images/71/71d018c5f48d3afb47781ddff61419623e8c6d15856151398905494498254880.svg)\n\nConsequently,\n![](images/57/57897701facaf26cf0826a2b6a12f408ae1acd8a5f4e78575a4457f66a156d9b.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/e6/e6090f180976846ce7a2e6e8d696a0fa078a471c950de6188908528c881839fb.svg) the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of ![](images/79/792ff38b4658d1d4bbfbc57cbe93be95dd8d90b24559f601250eaa81becf8d24.svg) we get\n\n![](images/99/99914dd947bc5c5acbb4d9aa989efb9fef4dc78b0242629ea0508a81e7058f61.svg)" :tutorial/title "Vectors in Opposite Directions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362453}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362453 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235285 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234976 :question/id #uuid "aeaa36a2-41b5-5b7b-9797-f39df1e34050" :question/math-academy-id "e-26144" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. \n\n![](images/d4/d4f18c3567d0c55d3d6e002fbe2c6ac50b97b7adc14b016c8e95006de98ca15d.png)\n\nGiven that ![](images/a7/a77e5fef143d51d984ce41363465af5f210291f6355b59da4302a944b6a49197.png) find ![](images/8a/8aef212f0b07b6c0fb04e3b3c0e415f3c93d698d48414bc0860a6405db20099b.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find ![](images/ae/ae4e47577b23025037443e79415dc50ff81b984cac5c4bb82421f985cfe4d69b.png) we want to match the expression\n![](images/bd/bd1ef582b76affe848465f42a9c78e897d93f1a2b535b7e880da8ab016561451.png)\n\nWe are given that one of the components is ![](images/dc/dc10081b11a8cfba96ed4d16d9ca1c6448af7f6198a5550955c2d123a657d339.png) So, we first draw the vector ![](images/7e/7efe1d8a24967d9509e73d84e1fb73eea1df1c2f113360cd3a34d8ef29df62e9.png) from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point ![](images/06/06046901b5ecb50bfedb17ffaf4c19cf690e50bd2e6e7c668fa59787ce4d71b7.png)\n\n![](images/65/65a6b5743ab9b6ef820744d0d6601b7dfd8de08a3f895661d96d2fe2cab8e0a7.png)\n\nLet ![](images/49/492123d15daf8306f0f5f893c7230d60f9eb8312d17b20f9a331f40f36b69b48.png) be the endpoint of the vector ![](images/d8/d8c5a62b30eeb1bc2e35d95dfc5d964dd00e512f3f7b1b53379755b1ebff5129.png) Then\n\n![](images/2d/2d7c2df783579edf0ca151150eeb55471b3f3fc2afc4e7836678b25670b484c4.png)\n\nIn ![](images/3e/3e50bb6908d0311cbf9fe09b7a8039c9d438eeda7d312f7fa87d6aa835523d49.png) we have:\n\n- ![](images/9a/9a61fb0fb1ada64abe8bd22d8b6f14a8248212de90bff583ebd1881bdf21efc5.png)\n\n- ![](images/af/af03af84c1a16b4d534cc63ac9a1b3d8d5b7d181b32cb23190deb192d4d8b8b1.png) and ![](images/d9/d9dc9bcbe34586fe4e368536ed74377c765274e2e5b008e1fee5d65e3b5008c0.png) have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n![](images/da/da5024a9515dce187468fd87b63acfd0fd89bfd867a9f03388782221604a3e74.png)\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- ![](images/cb/cb4b9bd2f50fd4b2da78369b90c522bc2b7ee20cbafa9460b643889733487c5b.png)\n\n- ![](images/5f/5fdd95564252a9a4218055ca7670a535233aa026955b7e410700e26c4f7d170c.png) and ![](images/1f/1f42d4df263db3845ca2361b8f89c4075e0cdb9b39e5564c39ae82115932a201.png) have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n![](images/da/da69cf085ea0816397356abf0878c798ab653c04fe34a51b8b849610d57778d4.png)\n\nAdditionally,\n\n![](images/57/5765a85c90552570f9a2a041f2f324a9fa0ce183d42708344531a13fbee802c8.png)\n\nConsequently,\n![](images/57/578802d62c9ab4cfbc8f250975b5b7be1396907593ba95901b8b21ad6a116d04.png)\n\nSince ![](images/46/466863f8c7522ac89896a4233a823be02940535097c28328fa2c9e9e1f4bc3d8.png) the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of ![](images/37/3720c5887713d3f7a29be384fe5e883390935b2b9b469af626a8dba968f4da8b.png) we get\n\n![](images/1c/1c2480355dc4e29a136a3bd7b6855e076f1c9698507523cafbd934521b91c6bf.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097487}}] :activity/title "Describing the Position Vector of a Point Using Known Vectors" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096886}] [#edb/ref 17592186048357 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111499} :activity/id #uuid "70a6ba48-4030-54b9-9b1a-89897e8b7cec" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111499 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111915 :tutorial/content "Two lines are **perpendicular** if they intersect at a right angle. For example, the lines ${\\color{red}y = 2x - 1}$ and ${\\color{blue}y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+5}$ shown below are perpendicular:\n\n![](images/1a/1a6eb1344ebbc9b41f58e7e68594d6913f4ce75a60ed0f0a3bd869168d088e08.png)\n\nWe can tell whether two lines are perpendicular by looking at their slopes. In general,\n\n> *If two lines are perpendicular, then the product of their slopes is equal to $-1.$*\n\nWe see this with the two lines graphed above: the lines ${\\color{red}y = 2x - 1}$ and ${\\color{blue}y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+5}$ have slopes $\\color{red}2$ and ${\\color{blue}-\\dfrac{1}{2}},$ respectively. If we take the product of the slopes, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2} \\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) }} = -1.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The only exception is for horizontal and vertical lines. Horizontal and vertical lines are perpendicular, but the slope of a vertical line is undefined, so we cannot multiply it by anything." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111500}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111500 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110599 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110334 :question/id #uuid "4cc37ad7-b382-5b7e-bcda-92b2818c5650" :question/math-academy-id "e-14359" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $y= -\\dfrac{2}{5}x-3?$\n\n- $y = 5x + 1$\n\n- $y = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 3$\n\n- $y = -\\dfrac{x}{2} + 5$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nThe line $y=-\\dfrac{2}{5}x-3$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $-\\dfrac{2}{5}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 3$ has a slope of $\\dfrac{5}{2}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n\\left( -\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{5}{2} = -1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 3$ is perpendicular to the given line.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Slope-Intercept Form are Perpendicular"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111501}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111501 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110600 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110343 :question/id #uuid "b5470454-6a27-5eda-a323-74ce90259f4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14734" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $4x - 2y = 10?$\n\n- $y = \\dfrac{1}{2}x + 3$\n\n- $y = -4x + 1$\n\n- $y = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $4x - 2y = 10,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y & =10 \\\\ -2y & =-4x+10 \\\\ y & =2x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $2.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5$ has a slope of $-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5$ is perpendicular to the given line.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Standard Form are Perpendicular"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111502}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110601 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110335 :question/id #uuid "a53a3337-108b-5129-b203-ff70ce8d0abe" :question/math-academy-id "e-14362" :question/problem "The lines $y=2x+9$ and $3y+kx = 12$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\n- The first line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=2x+9$ is $2.$\n\n- To find the slope of  $3y+kx = 12,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+kx & =12 \\\\ 3y & =-kx+12 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{k}{3}x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line $3y +kx = 12$ is $-\\dfrac{k}{3}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot\\left(- \\dfrac{k}{3}\\right) = -1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k = \\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining Unknown Parameters Given Two Perpendicular Lines"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111503}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111503 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110602 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110336 :question/id #uuid "5eca5ef2-627f-5d32-8edb-88e223b11d71" :question/math-academy-id "e-14365" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $x=2$ and passes through the point $(3,4).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the line $x=2$ is a vertical line, a perpendicular line would need to be horizontal. So, the perpendicular line must take the form $y=k$ for some value of $k.$\n\nThe perpendicular line needs to go through the point $(3,4),$ which has a $y$-coordinate of $4.$ So, the perpendicular line is $y=4.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Horizontal and Vertical Lines"}}] :activity/title "Perpendicular Lines in the Coordinate Plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110818}] [#edb/ref 17592186050523 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176859} :activity/id #uuid "5f43bfbe-a51c-5be9-8b78-730c73414b85" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176859 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177066 :tutorial/content "Let's look at the graph of $y=\\tan x.$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nThis graph also has infinitely many zeros, and there is an infinite number of asymptotes of the function. We can write down general expressions for these properties, assuming $n$ represents an integer.\n\n**The Zeros of the Function**\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the zeros of the function are $x=0,\\pm\\pi,\\dots.$ \n\nWe write down a general expression for the zeros as follows:\n\n$$\nx = n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Asymptotes of the Function**\n\nThe function has an asymptote when $x=\\dfrac\\pi 2$ and the function is periodic with period $\\pi.$ Therefore, a general equation for the asymptotes of the graph is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176860}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176860 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176049 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175695 :question/id #uuid "2f4571af-99cf-5a4a-9998-fb6a0f7d6be9" :question/math-academy-id "e-11004" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\tan x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The zeros of the function are at $x = \\pi n.$\n\n- The function has asymptotes at $x = 2\\pi n.$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-1,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\tan x.$\n\n![](images/32/326418398ce1a33f03a21f3bdb58107856f4ccb72241429f1604486281e60822.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The zeros occur at $x=0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm2\\pi,\\ldots,$ which can be written as $x=n\\pi.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function has an asymptote at $x = \\dfrac \\pi 2$ and the period is $\\pi.$ Therefore, the asymptotes occur at $x = \\dfrac \\pi 2 + \\pi n.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function takes values on all the real numbers, hence the range is $y\\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Tangent Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176861}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176861 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177067 :tutorial/content "Recall again the graph of the function $y=\\tan x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nWe've seen that the function $y=\\tan x$ is periodic, where the period is $\\pi.$\n\n![](images/e9/e911ee6c5bf6c178812c3079633544653066eb6371c4e6c38e3d2df51e622742.png)\n\n   The periodicity of $y=\\tan x$ means that $\\tan\\left( x+n\\pi\\right) = \\tan\\left( x\\right)$ for any number $x$ and any integer $n.$" :tutorial/title "The Periodicity Property of the Tangent Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176862}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176862 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176050 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175696 :question/id #uuid "f4649856-d777-5468-a7f4-df50479e36d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-11005" :question/problem "If $\\tan\\left( x\\right) = -2$ for some value of $x,$ then find the value of $\\tan\\left( x-2\\pi\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=\\tan x$ is $\\pi.$ Therefore, for any integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\tan (x+n\\pi).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡x & =tan⁡(x+(-2)\\cdot \\pi )=tan⁡(x-2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\tan\\left( x\\right) = -2,$ we get that $\\tan\\left( x-2\\pi\\right) = -2.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Tangent Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176896}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176876 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176058 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175704 :question/id #uuid "0b146642-914c-52ce-9939-86752e95dcd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11013" :question/problem "If $\\tan x = -\\dfrac{2}{3},$ find the value of $\\tan (-x+7\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan{x}.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\tan{x}$ is $\\pi.$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\tan{x}$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\tan(-x) = -\\tan{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\tan{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x+7\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x+\\pi \\cdot 7) \\\\  & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\tan{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{2}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\tan (-x+7\\pi)=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Tangent Function"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176896 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177083 :tutorial/content "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(-x) = -f(x).\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, tangent is an odd function! So, for any value of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\tan(-x) = -\\tan(x).\n$$\n\nOdd functions always have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This is indeed true of the tangent function, as we can see from the diagram below. \n\n![](images/fa/fa332c1b04ea9ce36e09fae4368d7964d21d3a36cadb115ea2e1e89518af543e.png)\n\nWe can use the oddness property of tangent in conjunction with its periodicity property to simplify trigonometric expressions. Let's see an example." :tutorial/title "The Oddness Property of the Tangent Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176876}}] :activity/title "Describing Properties of the Tangent Function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176109}] [#edb/ref 17592186049991 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176867} :activity/id #uuid "b5e5427c-d46e-5198-b0d9-c69a155fe586" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176867 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177070 :tutorial/content "Let's look at the graph of the cosecant function again.\n\n![](images/67/67463d29b77a6db10538bcf5956f9bb8a40a5d41a2b7741c6de52bd336bcc550.png)\n\nThe graph of the cosecant function has infinitely many asymptotes, and there is an infinite number of points where the function takes values of $-1$ and $1.$\n\nLet's write general expressions for these properties, by assuming $n$ represents an integer.\n\n**The Asymptotes of the Function**\n\nThe function has asymptotes when $x=0,\\pm \\pi, \\pm 2\\pi,\\ldots,$ etc. Therefore, a general equation for the asymptotes of the graph is\n\n$$\nx = n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Points Where the Function Takes a Value of $\\mathbf{1}$**\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function takes a value of $1$ when $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ Since the function has a period of $2\\pi,$ a general expression for the values of $x$ for which $\\csc x = 1$ is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + 2n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Points Where the Function Takes a Value of $\\mathbf{-1}$**\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function takes a value of $-1$ when $x=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ Since the function has a period $2\\pi,$ a general expression for the values of $x$ for which $\\csc x = -1$ is\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} +2n\\pi.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176868}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176868 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176053 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175699 :question/id #uuid "12dc1c50-69bc-54d8-8d60-a7fc144c9644" :question/math-academy-id "e-11008" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the graph of $y=\\csc x?$\n\n- The function takes the value $1$ at $x = -\\dfrac {\\pi} 2 + 2 \\pi n,$ where $n$ is an integer\n\n- The range of the function is $(-\\infty,+\\infty)$\n\n- The function has zeroes at $x = 0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm 2 \\pi,\\dots$" :question/worked-solution "Let's have a look at the graph of $y=\\csc x.$\n\n![](images/a9/a92d46319d381f0a8cab7823021b3b7e9b5c030ad964586b8e72129a2cd5abe7.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The cosecant takes the value $1$ at $x = \\dfrac {\\pi} 2 + 2 \\pi n.$ Therefore, statement I is false.\n\n- The range of $y = \\csc x$ is $(-\\infty,-1] \\cup [1, \\infty).$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- The cosecant graph does not intersect the $x$-axis. Therefore, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."} :knowledge-point/title "Describing the General Properties of the Cosecant Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176869}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176869 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177071 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the graph of the function $y=\\csc x$ once more.\n\n![](images/67/67463d29b77a6db10538bcf5956f9bb8a40a5d41a2b7741c6de52bd336bcc550.png)\n\nAs previously mentioned, the cosecant function inherits its periodicity from the sine function, so the period is $2\\pi.$\n\n![](images/ce/ce59afb77eb33880d7e92d1c294b872ec3af898c43a2af6816ef63fee64abd3b.png)\n\nThe periodicity of $y=\\csc x$ means that $\\csc (x+2n\\pi ) = \\csc x$ for any number $x$ in the domain of $\\csc x$ and any integer $n.$" :tutorial/title "The Periodicity Property of the Cosecant Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176870}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176870 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176054 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175700 :question/id #uuid "a1713375-241f-5a53-a109-3c3d0f917411" :question/math-academy-id "e-11009" :question/problem "If $\\csc x = 8,$ then what is $\\csc(x-8\\pi)?$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y = \\csc x$ is $2\\pi.$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\csc{(x+2n\\pi)} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-4$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\cdot (-4)\\cdot \\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-8\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\csc{x} = 8,$ then $\\csc{(x-8\\pi)} = 8.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosecant Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176899}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176878 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176060 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175706 :question/id #uuid "12eb5678-b62b-50ad-97bf-88768c4bf365" :question/math-academy-id "e-11015" :question/problem "If $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{7}{2},$ then find the value of $\\csc(-x - 20\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y = \\csc{x}.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\csc{x}$ is $2\\pi.$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\csc{x}$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\csc(-x) = -\\csc{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\csc{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-20\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-2\\pi \\cdot 10) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\csc{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\csc(-x - 20\\pi) = -\\dfrac{7}{2}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177086 :tutorial/content "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(-x) = -f(x).\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, cosecant is an odd function! So, for any value of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc(-x) = -\\csc(x).\n$$\n\nOdd functions always have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This is indeed true of the cosecant function, as we can see from the diagram below. \n\n![](images/cb/cb63f7c0930524e9f54a65aad6d74784c403a6255f723b879d76586ad8ea598b.png)\n\nWe can use the oddness property of cosecant in conjunction with its periodicity property to simplify trigonometric expressions. Let's see an example." :tutorial/title "The Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176878}}] :activity/title "Describing Properties of the Cosecant Function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176117}]}
